      "text": "To find the sum of the values of \\( x \\) that satisfy the equation \\( x^2 + 1992x = 1993 \\), we can start by rewriting the equation in standard quadratic form. The given equation can be written as:\n\n\\[ x^2 + 1992x - 1993 = 0 \\]\n\nA quadratic equation of the form \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) has roots that can be found using the quadratic formula:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\]\n\nHere, \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 1992 \\), and \\( c = -1993 \\). Plugging these values into the quadratic formula, we get:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{-1992 \\pm \\sqrt{1992^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-1993)}}{2 \\cdot 1} \\]\n\nFirst, let's calculate the discriminant \\( \\Delta \\):\n\n\\[ \\Delta = 1992^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-1993) \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 1992^2 + 4 \\cdot 1993 \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 1992^2 + 7972 \\]\n\nSince \\( 1992^2 = 3968064 \\), we have:\n\n\\[ \\Delta = 3968064 + 7972 \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 3976036 \\]\n\nNow, we can substitute back into the quadratic formula:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{-1992 \\pm \\sqrt{3976036}}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{-1992 \\pm 1994}{2} \\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\n\\[ x_1 = \\frac{-1992 + 1994}{2} = \\frac{2}{2} = 1 \\]\n\\[ x_2 = \\frac{-1992 - 1994}{2} = \\frac{-3986}{2} = -1993 \\]\n\nThe sum of the roots of the quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by \\( -\\frac{b}{a} \\). In our case, \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = 1992 \\), so the sum of the roots is:\n\n\\[ -\\frac{1992}{1} = -1992 \\]\n\nThus, the sum of the values of \\( x \\) that satisfy the equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-1992}\n\\]",
